# ---------------- Article XV ----------------
setp('§15.1.a', name='Provisional Status', text="Provisional status is a remedial constitutional status applicable only to an existing State under the remediation and devolution provisions of this Article; it is not an intermediate stage between Territory and Statehood. A State in Provisional status remains a State. Its constitution, government, courts, administrative functions, Assembly representation, social-state access, and Article I protections continue without interruption. Sitting senators complete their current terms, but no new Senate term begins for the State while Provisional status continues. The State returns to ordinary State status upon publication of two consecutive annual Statehood Audits with no material failure finding by any Monitor. Further transition support, fiscal coordination, and administrative assistance during Provisional status are defined by statute.")
setp('§15.2', text="(1) A Territory's elected governing authority under §15.1 may by resolution request a Statehood Audit for the whole Territory. Alternatively, a citizen petition — signed by a percentage of eligible voters within the Territory defined by statute at not less than 5% nor more than 15%, authenticated through the NVS using Citizen Voting Credentials, and certified by the Elections Panel — achieves the same effect. A defined geographic subdivision of a Territory may petition for Statehood independently of the remainder under the same criteria, using the citizen petition mechanism scoped to eligible voters within the subdivision.\n(2) Each Monitor conducts the portion of the Statehood Audit within that Monitor's constitutional domain and publishes an individual finding. The audit examines: free, lawful, and competitive elections; functioning constitutional government capable of State responsibilities; effective rule of law and lawful administration; substantial compliance with Article I; and other objective constitutional-capacity criteria established by statute. An overall failed audit requires at least two of the three Monitors to find a material failure within their respective domains. A single material-failure finding is published as a warning requiring remediation but does not constitute overall audit failure. All findings and dissents are published to the NRS.\n(3) A Territory that passes the Statehood Audit becomes a State immediately by constitutional operation. No additional political vote, legislative act, executive confirmation, or Provisional stage is required. The Statehood change is recorded to the NRS as soon as possible.\n(4) Statehood immediately creates two Senate seats. The Elections Panel assigns the two seats by public lot to two distinct existing Senate classes and administers a special election within 90 days. The first senators serve only the remainder of the assigned class terms; the classes and terms of existing States are unaffected.\n(5) Every State is subject to an annual Statehood Audit under the same Monitor structure and criteria. Judicial review is available for legality, process, jurisdiction, and application of the stated criteria; a court may not substitute its policy judgment for a Monitor's domain assessment.\n(6) Where active conflict prevents completion of an annual audit in a significant portion of a State's territory, the Monitors may publish a provisional audit-status finding for that cycle. A provisional audit status is unavailable where the State materially contributed to the conflict conditions.")
setp('§15.3', text="(1) An existing State enters the mandatory remediation track upon an overall failed annual Statehood Audit under §15.2.\n(2) A first consecutive failed audit produces an Early Warning and requires the State to publish a Remediation Plan within the period defined by statute. A second consecutive failed audit produces a renewed Early Warning and requires a revised Remediation Plan. A passing audit before a third consecutive failure breaks the sequence.\n(3) A third consecutive failed audit places the State in Provisional status automatically by constitutional operation. The State remains a State as provided in §15.1.a.\n(4) A Provisional Audit Council activates upon entry into Provisional status. The Council comprises two officers appointed by the Civic Consul through the standard executive officer appointment process and two delegates appointed by the State legislature. It reviews audit findings, proposes remediation plans, and publishes recommendations; it is advisory only and the State legislature retains legislative authority. The Council dissolves when Provisional status ends.\n(5) When the first sitting senator's term expires after the State enters Provisional status, the Elections Panel administers a citizen referendum on whether to pursue devolution under §15.4. If the referendum does not approve devolution and the State remains in Provisional status, the Elections Panel administers another referendum at two-year intervals thereafter.\n(6) An affirmative referendum proceeds through the negotiation and ratification process of §15.4. Absent an affirmative referendum, the State remains in Provisional status and continues remediation.\n(7) A State may obtain judicial review of the audit sequence and entry into Provisional status on grounds of legality, process, jurisdiction, or application of the stated criteria. Review does not authorize policy substitution for Monitor findings.")
setp('§15.4', text="A State may voluntarily restructure its relationship with the Republic through devolution. Whether entered voluntarily under this section or following the mandatory-remediation route under §15.3, the process after entry is the same: first, a State referendum must achieve 60% of votes cast with participation by at least 50% of eligible voters on the principle of pursuing devolution; second, upon approval, the State and the Legislature negotiate a restructuring agreement; and third, the Senate ratifies the agreement by 2/3 of full seated membership. The agreement specifies asset and liability allocation, transition timeline, constitutional status of successor jurisdictions, fiscal obligations to the Republic, and continuing obligations; it is published to the NRS and legally binding. A State that initiates devolution voluntarily retains full State status, including Senate representation, unless and until the restructuring agreement takes effect. A State already in Provisional status remains in that status throughout negotiation. Any State completing devolution becomes a Territory before any further constitutional-status change. Social-state protections and Article I rights continue through the transition. A Territory or defined portion of a Territory may merge with a geographically contiguous existing State, requiring a 60% referendum with at least 50% participation, approval by the existing State through its own constitutional process, a joint LM-JM assessment confirming the existing State's constitutional integrity is maintained, and Senate ratification by simple majority. A referendum for a defined portion of a Territory is triggered by a petition signed by citizens equal to a percentage of eligible voters within the defined portion — defined by statute at not less than 5% nor more than 15% — authenticated through the NVS using Citizen Voting Credentials and certified by the Elections Panel. The petition period may not exceed 12 months. Following certification of any referendum under this section, any party with standing has 30 days to file a legal challenge to the process or result. Where no challenge is filed, or upon resolution of a challenge that is filed, the window closes and the next step proceeds; a certified result may not be challenged on the same grounds once the window closes.")

# ---------------- Article XVI ----------------
setp('§16.2', text="(1) At ratification, the Republic formally notifies every nation on the founding register of its rights under this section; notification is published to the NRS.\n(2) From Day Zero until the close of the Transition Window under §19.2, each recognized nation may elect, through its own lawful democratic or traditional governance process for major governance decisions, either: (a) Associated Community — continuing as a fully sovereign political community in a consensual Compact relationship with the Republic under Article XX; or (b) Territorial Integration — entering the Republic as a Territory under §15.1, with the ordinary §15.2 Statehood pathway available. A Territorial Integration election constitutes the petition and confirmation otherwise required by §15.6, and the nation and Legislature proceed directly to negotiation of an incorporation agreement ratified by 2/3 of both chambers. A recognized nation that independently qualifies for a founding direct-State entry under Article XIX may use that transition mechanism while it remains available; this does not create a separate post-transition Statehood pathway.\n(3) If a recognized nation makes no status election during the Transition Window, the Republic treats it as an Associated Community pending conclusion, confirmation, or replacement of a Compact under Article XX. That interim treatment preserves the sovereign-to-sovereign relationship; it does not alter the nation's sovereignty, imply surrender, or constitute consent to Compact terms the nation has not accepted. Predecessor treaties, compacts, and lawful agreements continue according to §§16.3 and 16.5 until superseded.\n(4) The Republic may not prefer, discourage, or offer inducements toward either status and must provide factual information upon request.\n(5) Article I applies within the territory of each recognized nation throughout the Transition Window unless the nation, through its own lawful governance process, positively rejects that application and publishes the decision to the NRS. Following the Transition Window, the rights position is governed by the status held — §20.3 for an Associated Community or the ordinary Constitution for a nation that elected Territorial Integration.\n(6) While Article I applies under subsection (5), any resident who believes an Article I right has been violated may bring an Emergency Rights Writ directly to the SC under its original jurisdiction in §4.5; the SC must rule within 7 days and the extension and interim protective-order provisions of §4.5 apply.\n(7) Indigenous nation membership and Republic citizenship are compatible and independent statuses; no status election diminishes Republic citizenship, consistent with §5.1.\n(8) Election of Territorial Integration permanently ends the founding Associated Community or Compact status under this section. That former status may not be revived by reversing the election. If the community later becomes a sovereign independent nation, any future Associated Community relationship requires a new Compact under Article XX. A sovereign independent Indigenous nation may seek Territory status under §15.6 on the same basis as another sovereign entity.")

# ---------------- Article XVIII ----------------
get('§18.4')['text'] = get('§18.4')['text'].replace("The National Trust Administrator is a statutory independent officer whose independence is protected by statute; the enabling statute may not reduce the stewardship obligations or diminish the EM's annual monitoring authority.", "The Legislature shall provide by statute for the National Trust's internal organization, staffing, administration, and operational procedures. No statute may reduce the Trust's constitutional stewardship obligations, alter the designation or removal safeguards of this section, or diminish the EM's annual monitoring authority.")

# ---------------- Article XIX ----------------
setp('§19.4', name='Pre-Constitutional Classified Documents', text="Predecessor classified material is subject from Day Zero to the standards of Article X. The Legislature must establish an independent review mechanism within 90 days of Phase 4 activation to apply those standards systematically. Material satisfying Article X may remain classified only under Article X; material that does not satisfy Article X must be declassified and published to the NRS. Failure to establish or staff the permanent review mechanism cannot preserve a classification that is invalid under Article X or suspend the right to review. Until the permanent mechanism is operational, a lawful temporary mechanism exercises only the review authority necessary to apply Article X and preserve access to review. Any Inhabitant may obtain Supreme Court review of a determination concerning predecessor classified material.")
get('§19.5')['text'] = re.sub(r'\n\(6\).*$', "\n(6) From Day Zero, an independent judicial forum must remain continuously available for Article I claims arising from the nullification or displacement of predecessor law. Failure to establish or staff the permanent federal judiciary does not suspend jurisdiction or remedy. A temporary judicial mechanism may exercise only the authority necessary to preserve constitutional rights and ends automatically when the permanent judiciary is constitutionally capable of exercising the relevant jurisdiction.", get('§19.5')['text'], flags=re.S)
setp('§19.7', text="The joint Monitor panel assembles the initial Candidate Registers for constitutionally established positions before Phase 3 elections can occur. The panel performs the register-assembly and qualification-review function required during transition; it compiles the founding register of recognized Indigenous nations under §16.1 and publishes it to the NRS before the close of Phase 1. Registers assembled by the panel carry full constitutional validity and the Legislature continues them through the first budget cycle after Phase 4. The panel performs interim Monetary Authority certification functions from Phase 4 activation only until the Monetary Authority is constitutionally constituted and capable of performing those functions under §12.1.a. Initial MA staffing and selection must comply with the independence, candidate-process, confirmation, staggered-term, and removal safeguards of §12.1.a; no temporary transition arrangement creates or implies a constitutionally fixed MA Director.")
setp('§19.9', name='Required Institutions and Temporary Continuity', text="Where this Constitution requires a statutory independent agency or other permanent institution to perform a specific constitutional function, the Legislature must establish the permanent institution within the statutory period and the following constitutional maximums from Phase 4 activation: the civic proficiency assessment administrator under §5.1, 180 days; the census agency under §3.2, 18 months; and a constitutionally capable Monetary Authority under §12.1.a, 180 days. A lawful temporary substitute may perform only the functions necessary to preserve constitutional continuity or rights until the permanent institution is capable. Missing an establishment deadline does not enlarge the substitute's authority, make it permanent, or suspend the underlying constitutional obligation. A missed constitutional maximum is immediately justiciable; a court of competent jurisdiction may compel the responsible actors and may authorize only those temporary measures necessary to preserve continuity or rights. Temporary substitute authority ends automatically when the permanent institution is constitutionally capable of performing the function. The LM publishes a compliance finding when an establishment deadline is missed and when the permanent institution becomes operational. The Legislature may not permanently delegate a constitutionally required independent function to a Monitor in lieu of establishing the required institution. The Legislature must by statute establish the transition from predecessor voting infrastructure to the constitutionally required NVS, ensuring no gap in democratic participation.")

